    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Reflux esophagitis, responding (K21.00)</content>. Symptoms resolved on omeprazole with the meal changes. The gastric biopsy histology confirms no Helicobacter pylori and no gastritis, so no eradication is needed. [S]<list><item>Continue omeprazole 20 mg before breakfast to complete eight weeks, then stop and take it on demand for heartburn.</item><item>Keep the early evening meal and the raised bed head.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic insomnia, improving with CBT-I (G47.00)</content>. Insomnia Severity Index 12, down from 17, after two sessions and with the reflux treated. [S]<list><item>Complete the six CBT-I sessions.</item><item>No hypnotic.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opium-containing remedy, now on alternate nights by his choice (F11.90)</content>. A reduction he chose and made himself. The restlessness and poor sleep on the first nights without it were mild withdrawal, which settled; no other DSM-5-TR criterion is present, so the picture remains expected physiological dependence without opioid use disorder. No thoughts of self-harm. [S]<list><item>Stay at alternate nights for now; any further change is his decision, made slowly.</item><item>Never take a larger amount to make up for a missed night, since tolerance falls on the nights off.</item><item>Naloxone at the bedside; review at the next visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Strain of command (Z56.6)</content>. PHQ-9 5, down from 8. He now hands decisions to his deputy after the evening meal. [S]<list><item>Keep the protected evening hours.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Reflux symptoms: heartburn, chest pain after meals, regurgitation, night waking</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Resolved within a week of omeprazole</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Alarm features: difficulty or pain swallowing, bleeding, vomiting, weight loss, anemia</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None; weight up to 64.0 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Helicobacter pylori histology</td><td>At endoscopy; result at the next visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Negative; no gastritis</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Proton pump inhibitor: dose, timing before breakfast, and adherence</td><td>Eight weeks for erosive esophagitis, then the lowest effective dose or on demand</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taken before breakfast daily; four more weeks, then on demand</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Meal timing, head-of-bed elevation, alcohol at night</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Main meal early; head of bed raised; wine rarely at night</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Opium-containing remedy: amount, pattern, benefits and harms, his own goals</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Alternate nights for ten days, by his choice; same amount when taken</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Reassessment after a change in opioid use</td><td>Within one to four weeks of a change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed today, ten days after his change; benefits kept, brief withdrawal settled</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home and no alcohol or sedatives with the remedy</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>At the bedside; no alcohol on remedy nights</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>DSM-5-TR opioid use disorder criteria</td><td>At intake and when the pattern changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild withdrawal on the first nights off, now settled; no other criterion</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk and safety review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of self-harm</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 and GAD-7</td><td>At intake, at follow-up and at the end of the episode</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 5</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Insomnia Severity Index</td><td>At intake and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>12, down from 17</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (six sessions) with sleep diary</td><td>Weekly or every two weeks for six sessions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two sessions done; sleep diary kept</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Constipation on the opioid</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Regular with polyethylene glycol two or three times a week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Cardiovascular risk and statin decision</td><td>Every four to six years by the PREVENT equations</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Calcium score 0 with LDL 133 mg/dL and lipoprotein(a) 38 nmol/L: low risk; no statin; recalculate in four to six years</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A cardiac cause of the chest pain excluded within two weeks. Outcome by 2026-05-19: Normal ECG at intake; CT coronary angiography one week later: calcium score 0 and no plaque [S]</item><item>Chest and stomach pain after meals controlled, with appetite and weight returning. In progress; target 2026-08-25 [S]</item><item>Insomnia Severity Index below 8 within four months. In progress; target 2026-08-25 [S]</item><item>The remedy used as safely as he can: naloxone at home, never with alcohol or sleep medicines, and decisions about it his own. In progress; target 2026-08-25 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: ACG Clinical Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (2022); ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection (2024); CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain, United States, 2022 (2022); Behavioral and psychological treatments for chronic insomnia disorder in adults: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine clinical practice guideline (2021); Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2016); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hyperlipidemia and ischemic heart disease monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit as booked; fifteen-week review after the omeprazole course. Return for heartburn that comes back most days, difficulty swallowing, or any chest pain on exertion. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-28: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

